p></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"PapSum"><div class=3D"revvt"><h3 cla=
ss=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Paper summary</span></h3></di=
v><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><p>The paper un=
ifies safety and reachability reasoning during typing in a functional langu=
age. Variables in the context are read angelically or demonically depending=
 on whether they are used for must- or may-reasoning. Dependency between va=
riables makes reachability reasoning more complex, which requires two diffe=
rent constructors over typing contexts: "=E2=88=97" to concatenate disjoint=
 environments, and "," to concatenate environments with dependent variables=
. Correspondingly, there are two kinds of function types: one for functions=
 whose argument depends on its context, and another for functions whose arg=
ument does not. The paper presents a function calculus with datatypes, and =
then extends it with nondeterminism via a gen operator that generates rando=
m values. Type soundness is proven using a type denotation, together with a=
 fundamental theorem showing that the denotation of a well-typed term is in=
cluded in the denotation of its type.</p>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"Str"><div class=3D"revvt">=
<h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Strengths</span></h3><=
/div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><p>I did lik=
e the approach of having angelic and demonic modalities in a single languag=
e, rather than treating safety and reachability in separate frameworks. The=
 paper is well written through the first half, and the large number of exam=
ples makes it a nice read. It also includes case studies that show how the =
system is meant to be used.</p>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"Wea"><div class=3D"revvt">=
<h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Weaknesses</span></h3>=
</div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><ul class=
=3D"rm">
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>Motivation. The only motivating example in the introduction is Peano div=
ision. The introduction claims that prior work does not provide a "general =
type-based reachability framework" (p. 1), but it is never made clear what =
this generality amounts to, or what specifically previous work fails to han=
dle.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>Practicality. It is not clear whether this is implementable. It seems to=
 be a nice theoretical result, but its practical use is left open.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>Presentation. I can follow the paper just fine up to Sections 3 and 4, b=
ut from there on the formalism relies on some symbols and definitions that =
are not in the paper. This matters most at the argument for choosing =E2=8A=
=91 as the Kripke order, which appears to be where the main technical contr=
ibution lies (=E2=8A=86 and =E2=8A=87 are not enough). I could not follow t=
his argument as written, and since the demonic and angelic clauses of Defin=
ition 4.3 rest on it, I was not able to check the core of the paper.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>Examples do not match the rules. In several places the worked examples d=
o not follow from the rules as stated: the second example on p. 13 does not=
 type its first branch, although the first premise of T-Match requires it; =
the context on p. 13 carries a binding for y that is not needed; Figure 6 (=
b) appears to require switching a demonic parameter to angelic, which =C2=
=A72 disallows; and the claim on p. 23 that the base cases of merge type-ch=
eck does not hold, since nothing establishes that the inputs are sorted.</p=
>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"ComAut"><div class=3D"revv=
t"><h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Comments for author=
s</span></h3></div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml=
"><p>Sections 1=E2=80=932</p>
<p>p. 3, "A counter-example to the validity of this judgement is given belo=
w": the reduction shown here is weird by reducing the body of the let first=
.</p>
<p>Section 3</p>
<p>p. 9, "of base types (b)": what is the type of datatypes such as lists?
p. 10, Figure 4 header: the relations =CE=93 =E2=8A=A2 =CF=84 &lt;: =CF=84 =
and =CE=93 =E2=89=A4_X =CE=93 are not defined in this figure.
p. 10, T-Fix: what is the relation =CE=BD =E2=89=BA x, and why is it needed=
?
p. 10, T-Fix: the type of f is missing in fix f.
p. 11, "T-Let instead directly combines this 'duplicate-then-consume' patte=
rn": isn't this just regular typing, in contrast to affine/linear types?
p. 11, "we require all well-typed terms to be total": why?
p. 12, the rule for match-expressions: the sum in the =CE=93_i is weird to =
me. What does this mean? The partition of environments using sums is not ex=
plained up to this point (see also p. 13, "so we first partition the type c=
ontext using T-CtxSub").
p. 13, y:{=CE=BD: nat | =CE=BD =3D x =E2=88=92 1}: this type magically appe=
ars.
p. 13, {=CE=BD: nat | =CE=BD &lt; 4}: why not {=CE=BD: nat | =CE=BD =3D 3}?
p. 13, the last example: the first branch is not typed, but the rule asks f=
or each i to be typed (first premise).
p. 13, x:{=CE=BD: nat | =CE=BD &gt; 0}, y:{=CE=BD: nat | =CE=BD =3D x =E2=
=88=92 1}: this should be just x:{=CE=BD: nat | =CE=BD &gt; 0}, right?.</p>
<p>Section 4</p>
<p>p. 14, "is compatible": what is the meaning of "compatible"? After readi=
ng further I infer that this means that the values associated to common var=
iables must be the same, and free for the rest. This should be clarified up=
 front.
p. 14, r1 =3D r2|dom(r1): what is dom(r1), given that r1 is a set? Do you m=
ean the domain of each environment in r1 (since they are all the same)?
p. 15, the clauses for the demonic and angelic modalities: this is key and =
should be motivated much more. I have little confidence in checking that th=
ese are right, given the examples below.
p. 15, "This neutrality": neutrality with respect to what?
p. 16, r4: this is not defined.
p. 18, CtxSub: this rule is counterintuitive given the reading of T-CtxSub =
on p. 11, namely that if something is typeable in context =CE=932 then it i=
s typeable in =CE=931. The direction of =E2=89=A4 should be explained expli=
citly.
p. 18, "The operator context =CE=A6": what is that? It appears here for the=
 first time.
p. 18, "every term reduces to (at most) one value under any particular envi=
ronment": is this stated anywhere?</p>
<p>Section 5</p>
<p>p. 20, =CE=BBx.0 =E2=8A=95 =CE=BBx.gen_nat () + 1: the =E2=8A=95 operato=
r is not defined for expressions. Is this probabilistic choice? There is no=
 semantics given for it, which makes the argument that follows hard to chec=
k.</p>
<p>Section 6</p>
<p>p. 23, "The base cases (lines 3=E2=80=934) type-check": if you don't kno=
w the arguments are sorted, then you cannot prove that the resulting lists =
from the first two cases are sorted either, so the program fails to type-ch=
eck for that reason as well.</p>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"SpeQueAdd"><div class=3D"r=
evvt"><h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title" data-tooltip-anc=
hor=3D"w" aria-describedby=3D"d-rf-SpeQueAdd">Specific questions to be addr=
essed in the author response</span></h3></div><div class=3D"revv revtext fo=
rmat1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><ul class=3D"rm">
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>As far as I can tell, at first, the only sources of angelic types are th=
e match rule and the constructor rules, and there the angelic types only co=
ver singleton propositions. Everything else has to come from angelic bindin=
gs already present in the context. Later angelic types are introduced by ty=
ping random functions. Are there any other way to introduce angelic types i=
n the context?</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>p. 10, T-Match, third premise: i is free here, or the forall quantifier =
is outside (the one for the first premise?). It appears that e_i is typeche=
ck without information about y_i, is this ok?. Also, why is the notation d_=
j y_j different from d_i(=C8=B3) (parentheses)?</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>p. 12, "These sorts of precise types allow the rule T-AppOp to forward-i=
nfer constraints": the substitution of the variable for the value in the ty=
pe is not clear when the value is a datatype. Could you elaborate this on t=
he practical sense?</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>In Figure 6 (b), to apply x to g you need to prove {=CE=BD: int | =E2=8A=
=A4} &lt;: [=CE=BD: int | =E2=8A=A4]. Is this true? Section 2 states that t=
he modality can only be switched for singleton qualifiers or in the trivial=
 cases.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item-minus">
<p>Is the calculus implementable, and if so, what would a syntax-directed v=
ersion look like?</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div></div></div></div></article><article id=3D"r106B" class=3D"pcar=
