<span class=3D"field-title">Paper summary</span></h3></div><div class=3D"re=
vv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><p>This paper develops a simple r=
efinement type system that supports reasoning about (in)dependence between =
free variables in the context, as part of assigning types to program expres=
sions.  This allows the system to state and prove properties of safety (as =
usual) and reachability, i.e. that a given expression can non-deterministic=
ally evaluate to every value in a given type.  A semantics is given for typ=
es as formulas in a bunched implication logic, with the meaning of a given =
formula as a set of sets of environments, and the type system is shown to b=
e sound with respect to it.  The proof is mechanised in Rocq.</p>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"Str"><div class=3D"revvt">=
<h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Strengths</span></h3><=
/div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><ul class=3D=
"rm">
<li class=3D"rm-item">Viewing angelic and demonic choice as a resource that=
 can be reasoned about using modalities in a bunched type system is a nice =
idea.</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item">I think the idea is developed into a quite elegant ty=
pe system, with most of the key definitions being conceptually quite simple=
, but allowing for complex reasoning.</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item">The soundness result is machine checked.</li>
</ul>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"Wea"><div class=3D"revvt">=
<h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Weaknesses</span></h3>=
</div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><ul class=
=3D"rm">
<li class=3D"rm-item">It seems that a lot of heavy lifting when using the t=
ype system is done by the subsumption rules, which require unpacking all th=
e definitions of the semantics and are thus a little unwieldy.</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item">There is a restriction that all well-typed terms are =
total, and to type a recursive function: the first argument x must be of ba=
se type, its refinement type must be a demonic type, the refinement type of=
 the recursive call must be predicated on it's argument decreasing relative=
 to x (and therefore be entangled with x).  Thus, what can be asserted abou=
t, e.g., unary recursive functions seems limited.</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item">Some relationship with previous work is unclear.</li>
</ul>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"ComAut"><div class=3D"revv=
t"><h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Comments for author=
s</span></h3></div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml=
"><p>The main premise of this paper, that angelic and demonic non-determini=
sm can be viewed as a resource and reasoned about using a bunched type syst=
em, is neat, and the authors do an excellent job, through many simple examp=
les, of motivating how the idea plays out in terms of the sorts of judgemen=
ts that can be stated and proven.</p>
<p>However, I don't quite follow the first interesting example, on line 82.=
  Isn't this judgement provable?  It only uses the (usual) T-Lam and T-AppF=
un rules and the context-less T-Var rule.  Here, it seems from the definiti=
on of the semantics that the context:</p>
