<p>In any case, this illustrates the point that the subsumption rules, whic=
h are just delegated to the model, are doing a lot of work.  This can also =
be seen in the fact that the typing rules barely mention the modal base typ=
e comprehensions, all the reasoning about angelic and demonic choice really=
 comes through subsumtion.  It is already the case that in other refinement=
 type systems, e.g. Liquid Types, a lot of emphasis is placed on the subsum=
ption rule in order to get reasoning done, and here it is even more crucial=
 since it is the mechanism by which to achieve weakening, which is essentia=
l when dealing with the entangled variables.  So, although I think the type=
 system seems very elegant, it partly dodges the interesting question of fi=
xing reasoning principles for angelic and demonic modal types, and it is no=
t clear how much of it is novel compared to previous work on bunched typing=
.  Incidentally, although in, e.g. Liquid Types, subsumption is in part del=
egated to the model, but, in my opinion, the model there is much simpler, a=
nd thus one can reason about subsumption without having to carefully unpack=
 a lot of definitions.</p>
<p>The case studies of Section 6 are helpful in helping to imagine how the =
system may potentially be used.  However, I did not follow the first exampl=
e in Figure 6(a).  If the two inputs to f are allowed to be entangled, isn'=
t it possible to supply an input y that depends on x in such a way that whe=
never x=3D0, y&gt;10 (although both can, say, reach any non-negative intege=
r)?  For this pair of inputs, the then branch will be taken whenever x=3D0 =
and the else branch whenever x!=3D0, and thus, contrary to its specificatio=
n, the function will never output 1.</p>
<p>The incorrectness example seems to require typing the recursive merge fu=
nction with a type that violates the requirements on the type of fixpoints =
(e.g. the first argument must be a demonic base type and the left hand side=
 of a "normal" arrow).  Maybe this type can be obtained by subsumption?  Ge=
nerally, the restrictions on the use of recursive functions seem rather lim=
iting.  It is not clear to me what kinds of interesting angelic properties =
can be asserted for unary recursive functions.  Also, as far as I know, the=
re is no similar restriction to total functions in other call-by-value refi=
nement type systems, such as Liquid Types.  On the other hand, it seems cle=
ar that the system can be an alternative to Zhou et al's coverage checking =
type system.</p>
