s=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"PapSum"><div class=3D"revvt"><h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"=
><span class=3D"field-title">Paper summary</span></h3></div><div class=3D"r=
evv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><p>This paper presents a type sy=
stem for higher-order programs, with both overapproximating and underapprox=
imating refinement types, and its denotational semantics.
To mediate subtle interactions between angelic and demonic choices, which a=
re related to under- and over-approximation, respectively, bunched typing i=
s used.  The problem is that the values of different variables may have a c=
o-relation (or the paper calls it "entanglement"), which complicates reason=
ing about reachability.  By using bunched typing, it is possible to express=
 no-corelation and may-corelated between two typing contexts.
As a result, there are two kinds of function types: one that takes an argum=
ent that must not be related to the values in the context and the other tha=
t takes an argument that may be related to the values in the context.</p>
<p>The target language is a call-by-value lambda calculus with inductive da=
tatypes, recursion, and nondeterministic choice.  In addition to the two ki=
nds of refinement types and dependent function types, it has intersection t=
ypes and a kind of union types, which mean "both disjuncts are reachable by=
 angelic choice".</p>
<p>The denotational semantics of types is given via context logic, which is=
 an extension of BI with modality to represent demonic and angelic choices =
and can describe properties about a set of environments (of the same domain=
).  Finally, the type system is shown to be sound using this machinery.</p>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"Str"><div class=3D"revvt">=
<h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Strengths</span></h3><=
/div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><ul class=3D=
"rm">
<li class=3D"rm-item">
<p>A type system for a higher-order language with recursion and nondetermin=
ism to reason about both safety and reachability.  While overapproximation =
is more traditional and used to achieve safety, underapproximation is relat=
ed to reachability properties. However, mixing the two disciplines in a sin=
gle framework is very tricky, as the paper discusses.  This work overcomes =
the problem by using bunched typing to keep track of the corelation between=
 variables, as far as I know, for the first time.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item">
<p>A novel application of bunched typing.  Once one understands that the di=
fficulty stems from co-relation/entanglement between variable values in the=
 context, the use of bunched typing may sound straightforward because it ha=
s been used for phenomena related to "separation".  Nevertheless, this seem=
s like a new application of the idea of separation.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item">
<p>Definitions and proofs are fully mechanized in Rocq.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"rm-item">
<p>Writing is clear.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div></div><div class=3D"rfd0" data-rf=3D"Wea"><div class=3D"revvt">=
<h3 class=3D"s-rf-title"><span class=3D"field-title">Weaknesses</span></h3>=
</div><div class=3D"revv revtext format1"><div class=3D"rm rml"><ul class=
=3D"rm">
<li class=3D"rm-item">Algorithmic aspects of the type system (decidabiity/c=
omplexity) are not discussed. As a result, it's not clear how it can be app=
lied to automated reasoning. (Perhaps, it's more of one direction for futur=
e work, rather than a weakness.)</li>
</ul>
